# `function below(i, n) { return i < n ? true : false; }` copied in line and asked by `if (below(i, list.length))`: yes and no met at a join inside the copy, came back as one boolean, and the
# caller branched on it a second time; what the first branch had taught was forgotten at the join. A join whose ways bring yes and no to what its end returns out of a copy (or goes by) is walked
# once for each way, and the answers of the copy go on apart behind the call where the caller's block only branches on them: ONE branch is left. f2 = pick, f1 = below.
# RUN: walk f2 mechanisms=all
# CHECK-LABEL: code f2:Body
# CHECK: = Less
# CHECK-NEXT: Branch
# CHECK-NOT: Branch
# CHECK: = GetArrayElement
# CHECK-NOT: Branch
# -- source: yes-and-no-out-of-a-copied-callee-go-on-apart-behind-the-call.ts
